When a lazy image’s loading attribute becomes eager, Ladybird resumes
its deferred fetch without delaying the load event, as step 24 of
“update the image data” decided while the image was still lazy.

Blink does the same: ImageLoader::DoUpdateFromElement() fetches an
image it once deferred with SetLazyImageNonBlocking(), and the call to
LoadDeferredImage() that HTMLImageElement::ParseAttribute() makes for
an eager loading attribute doesn’t force blocking. WebKit does too,
with no failure expectation for the WPT test of this case: Its
ImageLoader::updateFromElement() marks a deferred image’s request with
setIgnoreForRequestCount(true), which keeps it out of the load event.

Gecko differs: nsImageLoadingContent::LoadFlags() picks LOAD_BACKGROUND,
which bypasses the load group, only while the loading attribute is
lazy, so the load that HTMLImageElement::StopLazyLoading() starts
blocks onload. Gecko’s WPT metadata expects the test for this case,
image-loading-lazy-below-viewport-dynamic.html, to fail.
